I'm re-ordering the issues for Andrew's benefit, because we could
use his help on (k).

On 7/2/12 11:06 AM, Ilya Goldin wrote:

 > k. Somehow handle discrete parameters, once Stan does.

There needs to be a way to indicate which parameters are discrete
in the input -- R's not too good at keeping types straight.

I don't even know what the effective sample size or R-hat measure
should be for discrete params.

I would think that we could use binary and other ordinal parameters
on their natural scale.  For pure k-way categorical variables,
we could look at the the marginals of each of the k outputs.
(That is, think of the output as a one-hot encoding, e.g., outcome
3 of 4 would be [0,0,1,0].

Andrew?

> It feels like we're arriving at a shared understanding of what mcmcplots needs to be able to do to serve as a
> visualization complement for Stan. It also seems imprudent to hold up the 1.0 release of Stan while the visualization
> stuff gets worked out.

The RStan releases can trail the Stan releases.
Everything doesn't have to be perfect or even complete
for a version 1.0 release, but we do want the plots
Andrew's interested in.

> My proposal is this. For Stan 1.0, we would release an accompanying mcmcplots (call it mcmcplots v. 0.5, since 0.4 is
> essentially ready) that provides the same outputs that mcmcplots already can but with the stan object as input. This
> seems feasible in the short term, and provides a reasonably useful subset of the wanted functionality (including
> density, trace plots, autocorrelation, and caterpillars). It will also serve as a test case for how we can work together.

I doubt you want to put a dependency on Stan into
mcmcplots.  Can't we just do all the glue stuff on
the Stan side?

> In later mcmcplots releases, we will change some plots and add additional ones, which will ideally benefit both Stan and
> the other MCMC engines that mcmcplots already handles. We should keep a running desiderata for future mcmcplots releases
> (perhaps as a wiki or bug tracker). This is a long and interesting list, to be clarified and prioritized in the future.
>
> a. Similar to R2WinBUGS, something like the right half of Figure C.2 here:
> http://www.stat.columbia.edu/~gelman/bugsR/software.pdf
>
> (This plot is basically a vertical caterpillar with 80% CI. In figure C.2 above, the chains are collapsed, but we would
> make this collapsing optional so that it's possible to juxtapose the 4 chains.)
>
> b. Diagnostic plots, for example plots of avg acceptance probability vs. log density, plots of #leapfrog steps vs log
> density, etc.

Not every plot we do in RStan has to come from mcmcplots.
But if you can add more diagnostic plots w/o dependencies on Stan,
I'd say it's a good idea.

These are all going to look like "parameters" in our output.

Often we want to scatterplot two parameters anyway to see what
their covariance looks like.

> c. On the overview plot for each parameter, a visual display of R-hat and/or n.eff. This could use visual cues to flag
> parameters with problematic R-hat or n.eff values, based on some threshold.
>
> d. The autocorrelation and partial autocorrelation plots could get omitted from the parameter overview by default.
>
> e. The autocorrelation plot could omit lag at 0.
>
> f. The autocorrelation plot could optionally separate chains instead of collapsing them as it does now.
>
> g. The autocorrelation plot could automatically flag the case of chains having very different autocorrelation. Is there
> a test for this?

Probably.  My hypothesis testing is weak at best.
We could always make something up.  We could, for instance,
use ESS as the basis of a test statistic.

We generally like to think about what would happen in a fully
Bayesian multiple comparisons setup (probably a useful tool to
figure out for CHI-like studies -- see Andrew and Masanao and
Jennifer's papers).

What we really want to test is R-hat.

> h. It would be interesting to add somewhat more intelligent default visualization behavior. For instance, if any
> parameters have problematic R-hat / n.eff, diagnostic plots would by default get generated for these, but not for other
> parameters.

I like that idea.

> i. Pluggable density function for the density plots.

This is low priority for us, but easy.

> j. Automatically choose decent density functions depending on the parameter type. I remember hearing that some density
> functions are especially appropriate for MCMC simulations.
>

- Bob

 > It occurs to me that I didn't formally introduce myself other than "I tinker with mcmcplots". My apologies. I'm a
 > postdoc at the Human-Computer Interaction Institute at Carnegie Mellon.

Nice -- I used to be a prof in the philosophy dept. at
CMU (which is where computational linguistics lived before
it moved 100% into comp sci).  I know Justine, of course.
I worked on HCI issues when I built dialog systems
at Bell Labs and SpeechWorks, and am very keen on good
UI design.

