On 7/2/12 11:26 AM, Ilya Goldin wrote:
> On Monday, July 2, 2012, Bob Carpenter wrote:

>     Ilya's proposing we treat the mcmcplots package as being
>     a source of MCMC graphics and that we integrate at
>     the level of RStan calling mcmcplots through R.
>     Pro:  This is the cleanest break as it means we won't
>     be collaborating on code in the same repository.  Cons:  It
>     adds a dependency to RStan we have to keep up with.
>
>
> Rather than RStan calling mcmcplots, I'd rather the user call mcmcplots, passing an mcmc::chains object, e.g.,
>
> denplot(stanobj, "delta")

Unless you want to roll mcmcplots into RStan, I think
it'll be cleaner in terms of software maintenance and
development to have RStan call mcmcplots for the plots
it needs.

I also don't think you want a dependency on a Stan object.
Unless R can work with the same kind of "concept" notion as
C++ -- that is, have the mcmc::chains object wrapper in R
implement whatever denplot(...), etc., require.

> It would be nice to have a plot.stanobj function that displays a default overview, but optional.
>
>     1.  Plots Andrew Wants (aka Version 1 Priorities)
>
>
> As per my message a minute ago, does it seem reasonable to have the Version 1 "spec" be simply consuming the Stan object
> and generating the same plots that mcmcplots already has, rather than tinkering with graphics?

Tinkering with the graphics is exactly what we want
to do!

>     A.  Everything in one plot
>     This is the one like the right side of the R2WinBUGs
>     plot.  We want to see at a glance the R-hat status,
>     medians and quantiles for all the variables (or some
>     of each of them for large multivariate params).  Right now
>     we're considering a horizontal layout like the left
>     side of R2WinBUGS, but with different scales per
>     multivariate parameter and problematic R-hat illustrated
>     with shading.
>
>     Modulo the sorting and fixed scale, this looks like it
>     fits the Wikipedia description of "caterpillar plot".
>
>
> We're very close to this now. The caterpillar plot is there, you can customize the quantiles, you can uncollapse the
> chains, and you can generate a caterpillar plot for each parameter group. A reasonable scale is chosen automatically for
> each param group. The only missing item is R-hat.

Nice.

>
>     B.  Traceplots
>     Traceplots for each variable, potentially with quantile
>     and median bars w. warmup highlighted in a different color.
>
>
> Traceplots are there now, but without quantile bars or warm-up highlighting. Is warm-up in Stan like adaptation in JAGS?

Yes.  It also includes what's often called "burn-in".  Andrew's
changing the terminology from "burn-in" to "warmup" in the next
version of Bayesian Data Analysis.

Quantile bars are not a priority, but warmup highlighting is.

>     C.  Diagnostic Plots
>     Given that Stan's adaptive and can have varying step sizes
>     and numbers of steps per iteration, plotting these for
>     accepted and rejected proposals.  No plans on how to do that yet.
>     We could follow Coda and treat duplicate param vectors as
>     rejections.
>
>
> mcmcplots doesn't have this now. This is very interesting to me and I'd love to think about this, but perhaps this is
> not mandatory for v.1?

It can't be a "must have" feature until we define it :-)

>     C.  Densities / Histograms
>     Across chains or combined chains (or both)
>
>
> Already there.
>
>     D.  Autocorrelations
>
>
> Already there.
>
>     One issue we're concerned about is scalability.
>
>     If whole chain data needs to be read into an R object to
>     compute statistics (as opposed to just displaying the
>     results), it's going to be slow and memory
>     intensive.
>
>
> True, but rjags does this for JAGS now, and it seems reasonable to want access to whole chains on the R side for various
> summary statistics anyway. We could use this straightforward approach for v.1, and optimize later.

It's also a HUGE drag on speed for JAGS.  Which is one of
the main things we're trying to address with Stan!

We do want to provide whole-chain access on a parameter-by-parameter
basis.  We just don't want to demand that all the chains for every
parameter get loaded into R before anything else happens.

If we have to do this for one reason or another (ease of coding,
serializability, etc.), RStan would be much less scalable than
Stan itself.  It's already going to be a problem if there's lots
of data as we're imagining that getting marshaled in R in most
cases (though we do have an option to read the data in from a file).

- Bob

