One reason that I said ggplot2 is not convenient to arrange to plots using ggplot2 might be that I am not that familiar with it.  But on the other hand, it is not easy as well in the traditional plots of R as we have
parameters that are scale, vector, or arrays.  But I guess if we are not that picky, it could be done.  I was not working on the plots for a couple of days, but I attached what is currently in RStan, for which Bob has a lot of good suggestions.  Soon I am going to work on it again especially as Andrew's opinions got exposed recently.

The first page of attached pdf is basically what is the right side of the plot of R2WinBUGS.  As Bob suggests, I might change it to horizontal and give an options to display the intervals for all chains, though I think it is a bit busy then. And more tinkering for the plots will happen after that.

One good thing about ggplot2 is that the user still could manipulate the plots after we plot them.  Assuming that sf is an object returned by function stan in RStan.  That is,

sf<- stan(file =,data =,n.chains= 4, ...)
ps <- plot(sf)

Currently the user then can do

plot(ps$par1) # par1 is a parameter ofthe model

to just plot parameter par1 (or any other parameters, or put plots for several parameters together in a plot).

Jiqiang

