Avg acceptance probs: 0.99 0.99 0.99 1

exp(1.7) = 5.5, so the inference for tau is basically the same as you got.

So the bad (or maybe I should say, good) news is that non-adaptive HMC really sucks for this problem.  Maybe I'll play around with randomizing epsilon and L at each step.  Or perhaps I should just fit a different model than 8-schools for the demo in my book.  In the book, I'm happy to be honest about the method's failings but I'd like the demo to work cleanly.

P.S.  here's the silly function I wrote to compute split R-hat.  I don't have the new n_eff, though; I'm waiting on all of you to create that!

monitor.split <- function (a){
  dims <- dim(a)
  n <- dims[1]
  m <- dims[2]
  a1 <- a[1:(n/2),,]
  a2 <- a[(n/2+1):n,,]
  dims.fat <- c(n/2,2*m,dims[3])
  a.fat <- array (NA, dims.fat)
  dimnames (a.fat) <- dimnames(a)
  a.fat[,1:m,] <- a1
  a.fat[,(m+1):(2*m),] <- a2
  return (monitor (a.fat))
}

P.P.S.  I reread Radford's chapter and it seems to me that when he talks about optimal efficiency, he doesn't at all take into account that computation costs are proportional to L.  I'd think this could make a big difference in the optimal acceptance rate even in theory.

