On 7/20/12 1:48 PM, Matt Hoffman wrote:
> RE:P.P.S.: I think Radford's assuming that you're holding οΏ½L fixed,
> and varying οΏ½ and L as necessary to ensure a reasonable acceptance
> rate. So cost is proportional to 1/οΏ½, and 1/οΏ½ has to grow as d^1/4, so
> cost grows as d^5/4.

I seem to recall two contexts.  One is to lower epsilon to get
a reasonable acceptance rate in all parts of the density and the
other is to avoid periodicities.

It's so cool that NUTS just does all this for us!

We're within epsilon of the BUGS estimates on all of
their models with NUTS (other than one parameter of one model, where
we're off by like 1/10th of a percent from the BUGS estimate -- Daniel's
still worried about that one and still looking into it).
We clearly never would've gotten that close with plain old HMC.
What's worrying is that it seems to be biased, not just noisy.

- Bob

