On 7/20/12 4:12 PM, Matt Hoffman wrote:
> This smells like a modeling bug to me. How else could we be getting
> log joints of 6.4e81?
>
> In particular, is this line right?
>
>        y[dog, trial] ~ bernoulli(exp(alpha * xa[dog, trial] + beta *
> xs[dog, trial]));
>
> It seems like there's nothing that prevents that probability from
> being greater than 1.

That's funny -- I said exactly the same thing when I first
looked at the model.  The answer is that each term in the sum is constrained
to be negative because alpha and beta are constrained < 0 and the
xa and xs are all positive.

The Bernoulli would also gripe and return NaN if the chance of
success param is outside of (0,1).

There's also the issue that the mass for alpha adapted to 2e-66
and the step size adapted to 4e+35!  Something's definitely
off in the model.  I'll take another side-by-side squint at it.

- Bob

